305-// --- judging a PR by the runs on its head (#455/#205/#95) ---------------------
306-// Arming is best-effort and can fail PERMANENTLY, so "arm and move on" is not a
307-// delivery guarantee. Two shapes, both observed across the fleet, both leaving a
308-// green PR open forever:
309-//
310-//   1. GATED CI. GitHub CREATES the pull_request run for a GITHUB_TOKEN push and
311-//      parks it at conclusion `action_required`, pending a human "Approve and
312-//      run" that nobody is watching for. The run never executes, so it never
313-//      reports; `enablePullRequestAutoMerge` then refuses the PR with "in
314-//      unstable status (required checks are failing)". Meanwhile the
315://      workflow_dispatch run started on the SAME head sha ran and passed. So CI
316-//      did pass; only the phantom gated run says otherwise.
317-//   2. AUTO-MERGE OFF. The repo never enabled Settings → General → "Allow
318-//      auto-merge", so the mutation is rejected outright no matter how green.
319-//
320-// Both are settled by the same evidence: the runs on the PR's own head sha. If
321-// CI has CONCLUDED and nothing actually failed, the content on that sha is
322-// verified and `auto-merge` means merge it. A gated `action_required` run is not
323-// a failure — it is a run that never happened.
324-//
325-// Deliberately conservative about MERGING, because that bypasses the arm:
326-//   - Anything still queued/running → `wait`. Never merge mid-flight.
327-//   - Any real failure (failure / timed_out / cancelled / startup_failure), or no
328-//     successful run to stand on → `close`: NOT a merge, and equally not a reuse.
329-//     (What `close` means is the CALLER's policy — a delivery closes and re-cuts
330-//     its per-cycle branch; a landing pass just leaves the PR standing.)

